@meta
id: 10-12
title: Which pair of brackets did you mean
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-paren, inner-vs-around
par: 66
minutes: 7
vscode: count brackets with your eyes, select, discover you were one out
open: refund-window.ts

@brief
Brackets nest, so "inside the brackets" is ambiguous, and vim resolves it
the simplest way: the innermost pair that contains the cursor.

When you meant one of the outer pairs, you say so with a count. `2i(` is
the second pair out, `3i(` the third. The count is not how many brackets —
it is how far out.

This module was changed to take `elapsed` as a number of milliseconds and
the two bodies were never updated. Both still compute the elapsed time
themselves from an `order` that is no longer in scope, and both hard-code
the day in milliseconds when there is a constant for it at the top of the
file.

Both `Math.floor` calls should end up as `Math.floor(elapsed / DAY_MS)`.
Nothing else changes: the constant, the signatures and the `t()` call stay
exactly as they are.

@keys
| `ci(` | the innermost pair containing the cursor |
| `c2i(` | one pair further out |
| `c2a(` | one pair further out, brackets included |
| `f(` with a count | land on the bracket you want |

@hints
Your cursor will land inside the innermost brackets. That is not the pair
you want to replace.
--
Text objects take counts. `2i(` means the second enclosing pair, counting
outwards from the cursor.
--
`2f(` on the first line and `3f(` on the second put you in the innermost
group. From there `c2i(` replaces the argument of `Math.floor`, brackets
left alone.

@solution
    3j 2f( c2i( elapsed / DAY_MS <Esc>
    4j 0 3f( c2i( elapsed / DAY_MS <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Fifty-seven keystrokes, thirty-two of them the replacement text typed
twice.

The two lines are the same edit at two different depths — on the second
one there is a `t(` call wrapped around everything — and the only thing
that changed was which bracket `f(` landed on. The object did the rest.
That is the property to trust: you do not have to find the closing
bracket, ever. Vim matches them for you and it does not miscount at
eleven at night.

The other half of the pair behaves the same way. `2a(` is the second
enclosing pair *with* its brackets, which is what you reach for when a
wrapper call has to go — although watch what it leaves behind. `d2a(`
inside `Math.floor((a) / b)` removes `((a) / b)` and leaves the bare name
`Math.floor`, because the name is not part of the bracket object. Removing
a call cleanly usually means `d2a(` and then `daw` for the name, or
`c2a(` and typing the replacement where it stood.

@start refund-window.ts
export const DAY_MS = 86_400_000;

export function refundWindowDays(elapsed: number) {
  return Math.floor((Date.now() - order.placedAt.getTime()) / 86_400_000);
}

export function formatWindow(elapsed: number) {
  return t('refund.window', { days: Math.floor((Date.now() - elapsed) / 86_400_000) });
}

@expected refund-window.ts
export const DAY_MS = 86_400_000;

export function refundWindowDays(elapsed: number) {
  return Math.floor(elapsed / DAY_MS);
}

export function formatWindow(elapsed: number) {
  return t('refund.window', { days: Math.floor(elapsed / DAY_MS) });
}
